{"id":21,"date":"2011-09-13T13:44:14","date_gmt":"2011-09-13T13:44:14","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/drumsandelectronics.wordpress.com\/?p=21"},"modified":"2021-01-06T12:21:58","modified_gmt":"2021-01-06T12:21:58","slug":"microphones","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/drumsandelectronics.com\/2011\/09\/13\/microphones\/","title":{"rendered":"Microphones"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><a href=\"http:\/\/drumsandelectronics.files.wordpress.com\/2011\/09\/3.jpg\"><img data-attachment-id=\"22\" data-permalink=\"https:\/\/drumsandelectronics.com\/3\/\" data-orig-file=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/drumsandelectronics.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/8\/2011\/09\/3.jpg?fit=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1024,683\" data-comments-opened=\"1\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"3\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-medium-file=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/drumsandelectronics.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/8\/2011\/09\/3.jpg?fit=300%2C200&amp;ssl=1\" data-large-file=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/drumsandelectronics.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/8\/2011\/09\/3.jpg?fit=605%2C404&amp;ssl=1\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" class=\"aligncenter size-medium wp-image-22\" title=\"3\" alt=\"ukko\" src=\"http:\/\/drumsandelectronics.files.wordpress.com\/2011\/09\/3.jpg?w=300&#038;resize=300%2C200\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" srcset=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/drumsandelectronics.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/8\/2011\/09\/3.jpg?w=1024&amp;ssl=1 1024w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/drumsandelectronics.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/8\/2011\/09\/3.jpg?resize=300%2C200&amp;ssl=1 300w, https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/drumsandelectronics.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/8\/2011\/09\/3.jpg?resize=768%2C512&amp;ssl=1 768w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" data-recalc-dims=\"1\" \/><\/a><\/p>\n<p>To explain how I approach the discussed subject, we\u2019ll start at the audio source, the drum set.<\/p>\n<p>Traditional drum microphones have a certain amount of leakage from their surroundings, for example a mic placed to record the snare would also record quite an amount of hi-hat.<\/p>\n<p>I want to be able to gain my mic\u2019s quite high, so that the mix between the processed sound and the acoustic sound can be controlled to a higher level, then the option of traditional drum mic\u2019s is not optimal. The presence of leakage would, in addition to not separating the sound sources fully, also present a challenge regarding feedback.<\/p>\n<p>I then suddenly stumbled upon the \u201cUKKO\u201d-series from \u201cB-Band\u201d. They\u2019re EMFit based contact microphones which you place on the shell inside the drum, right beneath the drum head. They sound a bit different from traditional microphones, but all the frequencies you want for a traditional sound are there, so it\u2019s basically just a matter of tweaking.<\/p>\n<p>Since they are placed inside the drums they offer exceptional isolation from the surroundings, and they can be gained quite high before there\u2019s any feedback.<\/p>\n<p>I got a five-peace set (they come with different preamps for the toms, snare and kick) for around 4-500 USD on ebay.<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"http:\/\/www.b-band.com\/ukko\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\u201cUKKO\u201d by B-Band<\/a><\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>To explain how I approach the discussed subject, we\u2019ll start at the audio source, the drum set. Traditional drum microphones have a certain amount of leakage from their surroundings, for example a mic placed to record the snare would also &hellip;<\/p>\n<p cla